In this lesson we'll look at the difference between random and non-random sampling, and why it matters for getting trustworthy data. The big idea is simple: in a random sample, every member of the population has an equal chance of being picked. In a non-random sample, they don't. Random sampling comes in a few flavours. A simple random sample is like drawing names out of a hat, or using a random-number generator. A systematic sample means picking every tenth name on a list. And a stratified sample splits the population into groups, then samples from each group in proportion. Non-random sampling is different because the chooser is deciding who to include. A convenience sample is just whoever's easiest to reach, like your friends. A quota sample fills a target, say fifty women and fifty men, but the picks within each quota aren't random. And a judgement sample is when someone picks the cases they think look typical. Random samples tend to be representative of the whole population, while non-random samples often carry bias because they reflect the chooser's preferences. Finally, remember that primary data is collected by you, while secondary data comes from somewhere else and may not fit your question perfectly. Now let's see it in action.